## Two Independent Choices

A project's **container runtime** determines where its packaged operations, composite evaluation, sidecars,
terminals, workers, and library image builds run.
Its **Pulumi state backend** determines where Pulumi stores infrastructure state.
Changing workload placement does not copy Pulumi state or move the Highstate project database.

Runtime settings include local and remote Docker engines and Kubernetes namespaces.
The default runtime is Local Docker, with the stable ID `local`.
The Highstate backend still owns authorization, project keys, planning, and recovery, even when workloads run
on another machine.

The Designer's **Runtime & Registries** settings page manages reusable runtimes, Pulumi state backends, npm credentials,
and container registry credentials.
Project placement selects a runtime and state backend from those settings.
The same workflows are available through the [CLI](/advanced/cli).

## Preparing a Runtime

Workload agents need an HTTPS runtime API URL reachable from their Docker engine or Kubernetes cluster.
This is a dedicated authenticated gRPC listener, separate from the public API used by CLI contexts.
The backend host must run that listener with a certificate valid for the configured hostname.
A private certificate authority requires the runtime API CA bundle in runtime settings.
Saving a URL does not start a listener or issue a certificate.

The integrated backend API enables its runtime listener when TLS certificate and key paths are configured.
Set these variables in the backend environment before starting the Designer:

```bash
export HIGHSTATE_RUNTIME_TLS_KEY_PATH=/path/to/runtime-key.pem
export HIGHSTATE_RUNTIME_TLS_CERT_PATH=/path/to/runtime-cert.pem
export HIGHSTATE_RUNTIME_PORT=7284
```

`HIGHSTATE_RUNTIME_HOST` optionally selects the bind address; the default is all interfaces.
Without an explicit port, configuring the TLS paths enables port `7284`.
Set each runtime's API URL to the externally reachable HTTPS hostname and port of this listener.
An unconfigured listener remains disabled, and configuring only one of the certificate or key paths fails startup.

Snapshot image builds also require `HIGHSTATE_RUNNER_BASE_IMAGE` to name a compatible runner image pinned by
digest, such as `registry.example.com/highstate/runner@sha256:<digest>`.
The runner image must include the runtime agent and the release-aligned Bun and Pulumi toolchain.
There is no implicit mutable base-image fallback.

Docker settings specify an engine endpoint and whether it shares the backend host.
Remote engine TLS credentials are separate from the runtime API certificate configuration.
Remote Docker and Kubernetes also require an OCI image repository for pushed snapshot images.
Kubernetes settings specify kubeconfig, context when needed, namespace, service account, and a rootless
BuildKit image for builds.

List and inspect settings, then check the selected runtime:

```bash
highstate runtime list
highstate runtime get <runtime-id> --output yaml
highstate runtime check <runtime-id>
highstate runtime check <runtime-id> --probe
highstate pulumi-state-backend list
```

The check reports the checks provided by the backend and returns a nonzero exit code when it is not ready.
The integrated backend checks access to the configured runner base image and starts a disposable workload to
verify its authenticated runtime API connection.
This requires `HIGHSTATE_RUNNER_BASE_IMAGE` and an existing project assigned to the runtime.
The probe uses the configured runtime agent binary and path, waits up to 30 seconds, and requests workload
cleanup after success, failure, timeout, or cancellation.
Cleanup failures also fail the check.
The `--probe` option requires an authenticated probe result, including when connecting to a separately assembled
backend; a missing probe provider is an error.
A successful connectivity check does not establish that every build, attach, storage, and networking path works.

### Creating and Updating Settings

CLI settings documents use protobuf JSON field names in JSON or YAML.
For example, a remote Docker runtime document has this shape:

```yaml [runtime.yaml]
meta:
  title: Remote Docker
spec:
  docker:
    engine_endpoint: https://docker.example.com:2376
    local: false
  runtime_api_url: https://runtime.example.com:7443
  image_repository: registry.example.com/highstate/library
```

Create it with the engine's client TLS files:

```bash
highstate runtime create --file runtime.yaml \
  --docker-ca-bundle-file docker-ca.pem \
  --docker-client-certificate-file docker-client.pem \
  --docker-client-key-file docker-client-key.pem
```

For Kubernetes, `--kubeconfig-file` supplies kubeconfig content.
`--runtime-api-ca-bundle-file` supplies a private runtime API CA bundle for either runtime type.
The CLI encodes these files for the API's byte fields; inline byte fields use base64.
Use `--file -` or a secret-file option with `-` to read that input from standard input.
Only one input can consume standard input in a command.

Updates replace the non-secret settings and require the revision returned by the latest read:

```bash
highstate runtime update <runtime-id> --file runtime.yaml --expected-revision <revision>
```

Include the complete intended non-secret configuration, including credential associations.
A stale revision fails instead of overwriting another edit.
Omitted write-only secrets retain their stored values; blank secret-file inputs also preserve existing values.
Explicit API clear fields remove supported stored secrets.
Secret values are not returned after saving; reads expose whether they are configured.
Deletes also require `--expected-revision` and reject settings that remain referenced.

The `pulumi-state-backend`, `npm-registry-credential`, and `container-registry-credential` command groups provide
the same `list`, `get`, `create`, `update`, and `delete` workflow.
Their `--credentials-file` option supplies a JSON credential document: a Pulumi environment-variable map,
an npm `token` object, or a container registry `username` and `password` object.
Associate credential IDs with the runtime that needs them.
Npm credentials select a package scope; container credentials select a registry host, optionally with a port.

### Changing Project Placement

Stop project operations, terminals, and workers and allow runtime cleanup to finish before changing placement.
Active or uncleaned workloads block the change.

```bash
highstate project placement \
  --container-runtime <runtime-id> \
  --pulumi-state-backend <state-backend-id> \
  --local-overlay disabled
```

Existing library images can be rebuilt on the new runtime from project snapshots.
Filesystem-backed Pulumi state remains in the old runtime's storage unless it is moved separately.
Selecting another state backend is a configuration change, not a state migration.

## Managing Project Libraries

Each project selects exact npm package versions, initially including `@highstate/library@0.30.2` by default.
Highstate resolves the complete dependency graph into an immutable **library snapshot** and builds an execution
image on the project's runtime.
The project database keeps the snapshot and lockfile; the container image is a rebuildable cache.
Reconstruction still requires the locked package archives to remain available from their registries.
On project unlock, Highstate collects unreferenced library snapshots and package versions.
Desired snapshots and retained deployment, evaluation, operation, runtime action, and unfinished build records
preserve the library history they reference.
Removing an image cache record does not delete its image from a Docker engine or registry.

The Designer's project library controls show selected packages, available versions, package sources, image
build progress, diagnostics, and local-overlay status.
The component list can filter by package and shows package-version provenance.

Backend hosts can override the initial selection before starting the Designer or creating the service graph:

```bash
export HIGHSTATE_INITIAL_LIBRARY_PACKAGES='[{"packageName":"@acme/library","version":"1.2.3"}]'
```

The value is a JSON array of unique package names and exact versions.
Use `[]` to start with no packaged library.
This setting initializes new projects and projects without a saved library selection; it does not replace an
existing selection on restart or unlock.
Packages must be available from the npm registries configured for the project's runtime.

```bash
highstate library package list
highstate library package versions @acme/library
highstate library package add @acme/library 1.2.3
highstate library package update @acme/library 1.2.4
highstate library package update-all
highstate library status
highstate library retry
```

Add and update require exact versions, not ranges or dist-tags.
Update-all selects compatible versions and validates the proposed set together.
Resolution, integrity, compatibility, or duplicate-type failures leave the previous selection unchanged.
An image failure happens after the new snapshot is selected; inspect `library status`, correct the runtime or
registry configuration, and use `library retry` to retry materialization.
Operations wait cancellably for required images and do not silently use the previous image.

Changing a package does not immediately change deployed infrastructure.
Planning shows package upgrades, and successful non-preview execution records the snapshot and package version
used for that deployment.
Preview, failed execution, and cancellation do not advance deployed provenance.
Refresh and destroy use the recorded historical snapshot when available, rebuilding its image if needed.
Authored instance models and project YAML continue to identify components by type without package fields.

### Local Development Libraries

The optional local overlay loads development definitions and sources from the backend's workspace.
It is available only with the well-known Local Docker runtime, not another runtime marked as local.
Remote Docker and Kubernetes cannot use this overlay.

```bash
highstate library local-overlay enabled
highstate library local-overlay disabled
```

If a local component or entity type conflicts with the project catalog, Highstate disables the complete overlay
and reports the conflicts.
For development on a packaged type, remove the conflicting package selection or use distinct component types.
Local units retain local execution and do not gain portable packaged provenance.
Local edits invalidate reviewed plans independently of package updates.

## Creating and Unlocking Projects Through the API

The public Connect `io.highstate.v1.ProjectService` exposes `CreateProject` and `UnlockProject` for backend API keys.
Send the key as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
Creation requires `project.create` and access to the selected container runtime and Pulumi state backend.
Unlock requires `project.unlock` for the project and works while its project database is locked.

`CreateProject` accepts a `project` with `name`, `meta.title`, and optional `spaceId`, `modelStorageId`,
`containerRuntimeId`, and `pulumiStateBackendId`.
Omitted placement IDs use the same defaults as Designer creation.
An optional top-level `projectId` reserves a CUID2 ID; otherwise the backend generates one.
The required `unlockMethod` contains `type` (`password` or `passkey`), `meta.title`, `recipient`, and
`encryptedIdentity`; passkeys also require `passkeyIdentity`.
The client generates an AGE identity and recipient and encrypts the identity with the password or passkey
before sending the request.
The response contains the canonical `project`, which is initially unlocked.

After a backend restart, call `UnlockProject` with `projectId` and the client-decrypted AGE identity in
`decryptedIdentity`.
When the backend runs with encryption disabled, `decryptedIdentity` may be empty.
Unlocking an already unlocked project succeeds without repeating unlock tasks.
Library resolution and image status remain available through the project library API even if initial
bootstrap fails.

## Upgrading Existing Projects

Database upgrades run forward automatically on backend startup and project unlock.
Keep a backup of the backend and project data together before opening it with a newer release.
Downgrade migrations are not provided.

The runtime upgrade preserves project IDs, keys, imports, deployed state, operation history, and logs.
It replaces backend library assignments with project-library storage and creates runtime placement references.
Old evaluation caches are invalidated and regenerated.
Package bootstrap saves initial exact selections before resolution, so a registry failure leaves the project
available for settings and diagnostics and can be retried on unlock.

Migration does not infer which npm version created an existing deployment.
Unknown historical provenance remains unknown, and those refresh and destroy paths require the retained legacy
executable environment.
Preserve that workspace and its implementation dependencies until those deployments have moved to known
snapshot provenance or have been destroyed.
Existing host Pulumi configuration is preserved as `legacy:host`; this marker is not a usable container state
URL and does not copy host state into a runtime volume.
Configure the intended state backend and arrange state transfer separately before packaged execution.

Old saved operation plans must be regenerated.
New plans carry a backend-issued review token, and launch rejects plans whose snapshot, model, overlay, options,
or phases no longer match the reviewed state.
Runtime-launched workers also need a runtime-aware worker SDK; the SDK's legacy connection mode alone does not
make an older image compatible with the new launch configuration.
